\title{\LARGE \bf Knowing Is Not Avoiding:\\
Neuro-Symbolic Hazard Election for Frozen\\Vision-Language-Action Policies}

% Double-blind: no author information.
\author{Anonymous submission to the IROS 2026 Workshop on Embodied\\
Neuro-Symbolic AI for Reliable and Safe Robotics (ReS AI)}

\graphicspath{{figs/}}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A safety filter is conventionally factored into a certificate, a fallback policy, a monitor, and an intervention scheme. All four presuppose that the hazard has already been named. For vision-language-action policies, naming it is the hard part --- and we show it is neither elicitable from the policy nor installable by prompting. On LIBERO-Safety, a safety-finetuned \pifive{} policy linearly encodes the hazard at the exact residual-stream position its action head consumes (AUC 0.998 on the suite whose safe/unsafe instructions are byte-identical, so the signal is purely visual), yet answers 0\% on ten pre-registered \emph{perception} controls, and still drives into it. Across 115 cells of instruction-level intervention --- seven rewriter models on one benchmark and four on the other, two evaluation stacks --- not one cell reduces hazard contact. We therefore place the safety decision outside the policy, in a fixed three-literal first-order rule whose literals a VLM grounds by answering only local per-object questions, and whose output drives training-free noise-space steering of the frozen flow policy under a discrete-CBF chain re-verified on the executed actions. Every part of the division of labor is measured: symbolic election matches privileged ground-truth identity (all $p\ge0.18$); deleting any single literal costs 17--29 task-success points or \emph{raises} violations; and both ways of granting the VLM more authority fail, and fail unsafe. The stack beats two shield architectures by $+15$--$20$ macro task success at equal-or-lower violations. Finally, we report a defect our own numbers led us to: LIBERO-Safety's \texttt{CheckRobotContact} predicate compares geom \emph{indices} against geom \emph{names} and so returns false unconditionally --- the benchmark's robot--hazard metric has never fired. We give an executable proof, a one-line fix, and an honest account of which of our claims it retracts.
\end{abstract}

\begin{figure*}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_teaser.pdf}
\caption{\textbf{Knowing is not avoiding.} One LIBERO-Safety episode, same instruction, same initial state. \textbf{Top:} the frozen $\pi_{0.5}$ policy --- which encodes this hazard at AUC 0.998 at its own action interface (Table~\ref{tab:probe}) --- closes on the mug, contacts the human hand at $t{=}573$, and fails the task. \textbf{Bottom:} the same frozen weights with symbolic hazard election driving noise-space steering: the hand is elected by the $\textsc{Protected}$ literal, a barrier compiles around it, and the policy routes around the hand to complete the task at $t{=}509$. No retraining and no privileged simulator state.}
\label{fig:teaser}
\end{figure*}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models are emerging as a foundation for general-purpose robotic control, with a single policy performing diverse manipulation across household, industrial, and other unstructured environments. The recipe is now standard: a pretrained vision-language backbone supplies semantic grounding, and an action head --- increasingly a diffusion or flow-matching head, as in $\pi_{0.5}$~\cite{pi05} --- decodes continuous motor commands. The resulting artifacts are enormous, trained on data no one has audited, and largely uninterpretable: billions of parameters mapping pixels and a sentence to a chunk of end-effector deltas, with no intermediate representation a human can read and no place to attach a guarantee. As these policies move from benchmarks toward deployment, task completion stops being an adequate criterion for correct behavior. The robot must also finish without injuring a person, damaging its surroundings, contaminating what it touches, or destabilizing what it carries.

What counts as unsafe, however, is not a property of the motion. A welding cell can be given a fixed keep-out volume; a robot that accepts arbitrary instructions in arbitrary kitchens needs something closer to the commonsense a person brings to an unfamiliar room. Consider moving a filled mug across a counter: the same straight-line transport is benign over bare countertop, hazardous over an open laptop, and a different risk again when a person's hand enters along the path. Our benchmarks contain a sharper version. In one task a human hand holding a banana rests near the reach path and any contact is a violation; in another the instruction is \emph{``pick up the banana and put it on the plate in my hand,''} where the same object, held the same way, is now the destination, and a keep-out volume around it makes the task impossible. No geometric predicate separates these cases, and no amount of collision-avoidance training separates them either, because the motions are the same. The only distinguishing signal is whether the instruction \emph{refers} to the object --- a semantic fact resolved at runtime, per episode, and revisable mid-episode when a person reaches in. Safety here demands runtime \emph{understanding} of what the scene contains and what role each entity plays, then runtime \emph{enforcement} against a specification that did not exist until the instruction arrived.

In principle this is what safety filters are for. Hamilton--Jacobi reachability computes the states from which failure can be avoided under known dynamics~\cite{mitchell2005hj}; control barrier functions enforce forward invariance of a predefined safe set~\cite{ames2017cbf,ames2019cbf}; model-predictive shielding replaces actions predicted over a horizon to lead to failure~\cite{bastani2021shielding}. The unified view factors any such filter into a certificate, a fallback policy, a monitor, and an intervention scheme~\cite{hsu2024safetyfilter}. All four modules take the failure set as \emph{given}, and that is what open-world manipulation withholds. The state is high-dimensional and partially observed, with object identities and extents recovered from pixels rather than read from a simulator; dynamics for manipulated objects are usually unavailable and hopeless for deformables; and the constraint itself resists analytic specification. One actual safety condition from our benchmark is \emph{grasp the knife by the handle, not the blade}: written formally, a predicate over a hand-enumerated list of twenty-four mesh geometry indices on a single knife asset, which does not transfer to the next knife and cannot be authored by anyone without the mesh in front of them. Recent latent safety filters relax the fixed-constraint assumption, synthesizing certificates inside a learned world model and even parameterizing the constraint at runtime~\cite{nakamura2025latent,anysafe} --- but a human still supplies what to avoid. Hand-authoring failure sets for every object a household robot meets is not an engineering-effort problem; it is the wrong shape of solution.

Since a VLA already consumes language, the cheapest imaginable fix is to write a safer instruction --- name the hazard, forbid contact, and let the model's own semantic competence do the rest. This deserves an answer rather than a dismissal, because if it worked none of the machinery below would be needed. We ran it exhaustively: 115 cells of instruction rewriting across eleven rewriter models (seven on one benchmark, four on the other), three prompt variants, four suites, and two evaluation stacks. Not one cell reduces hazard contact (Sec.~\ref{sec:gap}). The same holds for pushing safety into the weights: the benchmark we evaluate on ships a $\pi_{0.5}$ policy fine-tuned on 19.6k safety demonstrations, and it still contacts the hazard in 4.7--5.3\% of episodes on the suites where the violation metric functions. The mechanism behind the null is stronger than the null itself. On these weights the hazard is linearly decodable at AUC 0.998 at the exact residual-stream position the action head reads, while the policy answers 0\% on ten pre-registered perception controls. The information is present, unspeakable, and unenacted: knowing is not avoiding (Fig.~\ref{fig:teaser}).

Existing responses fall into three families, and they share a defect that is easy to miss. \emph{Analytic filters} give real guarantees but must be handed a failure set. \emph{Learned safety critics} sidestep specification by labelling rollouts safe or unsafe and training a value function or classifier to drive the same constrained optimization~\cite{safediffuser,pacs}. \emph{VLM-based reasoning} supplies the open-vocabulary commonsense the first two lack, but its output is textual and descriptive, and the gap between a paragraph about what looks dangerous and a corrected motor command is where these systems fail~\cite{brunke2025semantic,aegis}. What all three share downstream is that they \emph{repair}: a violating action is projected, corrected, or replaced, whether after decoding or inside it. Repair moves the action off the manifold the policy was trained on, and the cost is liveness --- on our long-horizon suites every barrier-class shield stalls, dropping task success from 58.0 to 27.5 while the step cap does not help (Table~\ref{tab:board}), a degradation reached independently by~\cite{pacs}. Notably, moving the repair from after decoding to inside the denoising loop does not fix this; the intervention point is not the axis that matters.

We keep the VLM and close the grounding gap with an \emph{intermediate symbolic representation}, then replace repair with search. The key observation is an asymmetry in what a VLM is good for: asked \emph{``what in this scene is dangerous?''} an API-strength model is right 47--84\% of the time, while asked \emph{``is this object referred to by the instruction?''} it is right essentially always (Fig.~\ref{fig:vlmauth}). So we never ask it what is dangerous. It answers only local, checkable, per-object questions, and a fixed three-literal first-order rule composes those answers into a hazard election. First-order logic is what makes that composition auditable rather than merely structured: the rule reads in one line, every clause is separately falsifiable, and deleting any single literal costs 17--29 task-success points or \emph{raises} violations (Sec.~\ref{sec:ablation}) --- a claim no prompt-shaped output schema can even state, and one that matters because letting a VLM compose the rule instead fails \emph{open}, firing on nothing (Sec.~\ref{sec:negative}). Because the rule's output is a named entity with a position and an extent, it is exactly the object a classical filter always assumed it was given, and everything downstream is textbook discrete-CBF machinery. Enforcement then searches the frozen policy's noise preimage rather than correcting its output, so every emitted action stays on the policy manifold, and a certificate re-checked on the executed prefix makes soundness a property of the actions rather than of the search that produced them.

\textbf{Contributions.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{A representation--action gap, within one checkpoint} (Sec.~\ref{sec:gap}): 0.998 decodability at the action interface, 0\% verbalization, and a 115-cell null on language-level intervention.
\item \emph{A neuro-symbolic hazard-election layer} (Sec.~\ref{sec:method}): a fixed three-literal rule over VLM-grounded predicates, statistically indistinguishable from privileged ground-truth identity in paired rollouts (all $p\ge0.18$), with two interchangeable grounding backends agreeing on all 59 scenes tested.
\item \emph{Causal necessity of every literal} (Sec.~\ref{sec:ablation}): single-clause deletion costs 17--29 task-success points or \emph{raises} violations; geometry alone is not merely weaker but less safe.
\item \emph{Search rather than repair} (Sec.~\ref{sec:enforce}): noise-space steering under a checked prefix certificate, recovering $+15$--$19$ task success on long-horizon suites, where every barrier-class shield stalls.
\item \emph{A benchmark reliability finding} (Sec.~\ref{sec:defect}): LIBERO-Safety's \texttt{CheckRobotContact} predicate cannot fire. Executable proof, one-line fix, and an honest account of which of our own claims it retracts.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.86\textwidth]{fig_scenes.pdf}
\caption{\textbf{What the literals have to resolve.} (a) A distractor adjacent to the target: separable only by $\textsc{Mentioned}$, since geometry alone cannot tell a hazard from the goal. (b) A human hand entering mid-episode --- the case $\textsc{Moving}$ exists for, grounded from displacement rather than a runtime model call. (c) A SafeLIBERO scene where the hazard sits on the reach path, engaging $\textsc{NearPath}$. (d) A certified rollout: task complete, zero hazard contact.}
\label{fig:scenes}
\end{figure*}

We organize prior work along the two axes the introduction separates: where the failure set comes from, and how enforcement acts on the policy.

\textbf{Safety filters and where the safe set comes from.} Beyond the classical filters of Sec.~\ref{sec:intro}, a recent line lifts them to high-dimensional observations by synthesizing the certificate inside a learned world model: latent safety filters~\cite{nakamura2025latent} perform reachability in a generative model's latent space, with epistemic uncertainty conformally calibrated to catch out-of-distribution hazards, and follow-up work learns smooth latent CBFs under hard-to-model constraints~\cite{nakamura2025latentcbf}. AnySafe~\cite{anysafe} parameterizes the constraint itself, conditioning the filter on an encoding of a constraint \emph{image} so that it adapts at runtime. That line and ours make opposite bets: they place the intelligence in a \emph{learned} safe set and leave identification implicit, whereas we keep the barrier deliberately textbook and place the intelligence in an \emph{inspectable symbolic} election rule. The distinction we care about most is the one that motivates this paper: even AnySafe adapts \emph{which} human-supplied constraint is active, while we \emph{derive} the constraint from the instruction and the scene with no human in the loop at deployment.

\textbf{Learning the safety model instead of specifying it.} The second family of Sec.~\ref{sec:intro} replaces specification with supervision --- labelling rollouts safe or unsafe and fitting a value function, classifier, or learned barrier that then drives the same constrained optimization~\cite{nakamura2025latentcbf,safediffuser}. The appeal is that it inherits open-world coverage from data rather than from an author. We report our own instance as a negative result (Sec.~\ref{sec:negative}): a consequence critic that predicts hazard contact at AUC 0.93--0.999 offline yields no rollout gain over the ten-line geometric gate it was built to replace. Our reading is not that learned safety models are the wrong idea, but that at this data scale prediction quality was never the binding constraint --- which is the empirical case for spending the modelling budget on identification instead.

\textbf{Semantic constraints from vision-language models.} The third family supplies the open-vocabulary commonsense the first two lack. Closest in spirit is work that compiles semantic scene understanding into motion safeguards~\cite{brunke2025semantic}; related systems steer frozen policies with VLM reward signals~\cite{vls}. The recurring difficulty is that VLM output is textual and descriptive, and the distance between a paragraph about what looks dangerous and a corrected motor command is where these systems fail. Our departure is to narrow what the VLM is asked for: only local per-object predicates, never a scene-level hazard judgment --- a restriction Sec.~\ref{sec:negative} shows is load-bearing rather than conservative.

\textbf{Neuro-symbolic safety for VLA policies.} Our closest predecessor is also explicitly neuro-symbolic and also evaluates on SafeLIBERO: English et al.~\cite{sota2607} interleave symbolic constraint satisfaction with neural trajectory generation, imposing a min-norm discrete-CBF correction \emph{inside} \pifive{}'s flow-matching denoising and reporting 82.8\% collision avoidance at 81.6\% task success. We reimplement it as a board row (Table~\ref{tab:board}). The disagreement is precisely about \emph{where the symbols belong}: their symbolic content is the projection operator, with hazard identity supplied by the simulator; ours is the election rule, with enforcement kept textbook.

\textbf{Identification without a certificate.} ``Your Model Already Knows''~\cite{yourmodel} reads a small set of VLA attention heads to localize the object the policy intends to approach, feeding a CBF without extra training. It is the direct competitor to our identification layer --- neural read-out versus symbolic election --- and it raises an objection we take seriously: VLM-based obstacle identification is too slow for the control loop and can only be invoked at episode initialization, leaving the filter blind to moving obstacles. Our answer is architectural rather than computational. The VLM is queried once per task and cached; the literal that handles dynamics, $\textsc{Moving}$, is grounded from proprioceptive displacement and re-evaluated at every replan, so intruder tracking costs nothing at runtime (Fig.~\ref{fig:scenes}b).

\textbf{Repair versus search.} On the enforcement axis, most safety mechanisms \emph{repair}: a violating action is projected, corrected, or replaced, either after decoding~\cite{aegis} or during it~\cite{safediffuser,sota2607}. PACS~\cite{pacs} observes that such mechanisms ``alter actions in ways unseen during training, causing unpredictable behavior and performance degradation,'' and responds with path-consistent filtering for diffusion policies; that is the Sec.~\ref{sec:enforce} finding reached from the opposite direction, and we regard the convergence as corroboration rather than competition. Steering a frozen flow policy through its initial noise is meanwhile an active area, but for \emph{performance}: flow-reversal steering maps suboptimal actions back to latent noise and onto nearby generalist modes~\cite{frs}, and VLS injects VLM reward gradients with Feynman--Kac resampling into frozen \pifive{} denoising~\cite{vls}. We use that family of operators for safety, under a certificate --- which is what lets us keep every emitted action on the policy manifold rather than paying the repair tax.

\textbf{Benchmarks.} We evaluate on SafeLIBERO~\cite{sota2607} and LIBERO-Safety~\cite{liberosafety} (Fig.~\ref{fig:scenes}). A recent survey of VLA safety names benchmark development that ``has not kept pace with model capability advances'' as an open problem~\cite{vlasurvey}; Sec.~\ref{sec:defect} is a concrete instance.

\section{The Representation--Action Gap}
\label{sec:gap}

All measurements in this section are on one checkpoint, \pifive{} fine-tuned on LIBERO-Safety, over that benchmark's seed-matched SAFE/UNSAFE twins (15 twins $\times$ 10 initial states $=$ 150 rollouts per cell).

\textbf{The hazard is there, at the action interface.} We fit layerwise linear probes at three token positions and report the \emph{pre-action} position --- the residual-stream slot that \texttt{action\_out\_proj} consumes --- under twin-grouped 5-fold cross-validation. Table~\ref{tab:probe} gives the peak AUC with both controls computed for every cell. Read the obstacle suites first: their SAFE and UNSAFE instructions are byte-identical, so length-only AUC is exactly $0.500$ and no textual shortcut exists, yet the hazard decodes at $0.940$ and $0.998$ from pixels alone. The affordance suite is excluded from this claim and we say why in the table: there the label is 94\% predictable from instruction token count, an artifact of how the twins were authored that binds anyone probing this benchmark.

