\end{table}

\textbf{The policy cannot say it.} The same checkpoint scores 0\% (95\% CI $[0.0,3.7]$, $n{=}100$) on ten pre-registered perception questions --- ``is there a robot arm in this image?'', not safety questions. Its text readout has drifted off the vocabulary manifold entirely (mean ASCII fraction $0.00$), because \pifive{} applies no text loss during action fine-tuning. Neither the base nor the LIBERO-only nor the safety-finetuned variant recovers the channel. This is not a limitation we work around; it is the reason no verbalization-based monitor, self-critique loop, or chain-of-thought safety check is available on these weights at all.

\textbf{Language does not install the behavior either.} We climbed the cheapest rung of the intervention ladder exhaustively: rewriting the instruction to name the hazard and forbid contact, across 7 rewriter models $\times$ 3 prompt variants $\times$ 4 suites at $n{=}150$/cell on LIBERO-Safety (84 cells), then replicating on SafeLIBERO with a different checkpoint, a different evaluation stack and a different metric family at $n{=}200$/cell (31 cells). \emph{No cell reduces hazard contact}: obstacle avoidance 1.3\% baseline vs.\ 0.7--4.0\% rewritten; obstacle+hand 16.0\% vs.\ 16.0--19.3\%; human safety 32.0\% vs.\ 24.0--38.7\%; affordance 57.5\% vs.\ 53.3--65.0\%. Every interval overlaps its baseline, and 0 of 31 replication cells separate in either direction. Task success moves in \emph{opposite} directions on different suites, which is itself the reason to make no directional claim about rewriting.\footnote{Discipline note: at $n{=}150$ the Wilson half-width is $\approx\pm7$ points, so we make no head-to-head ranking of rewriter models anywhere and never name a best rewriter. Cells withheld for exhausted API credits are labelled as such, never rendered as zeros.}

\textbf{What this licenses.} One thing, and it is the premise of the rest of the paper: the safety decision must be made \emph{outside} the policy and enforced \emph{on} it. It cannot be elicited from the policy's language channel, because there isn't one, and it cannot be installed by conditioning. That is a strong argument for an explicit symbolic layer, and a strong argument against the architecture in which a large neural model is trusted to notice the hazard on its own.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.98\textwidth]{fig_pipeline.pdf}
\caption{\textbf{The division of labor.} A LIBERO-Safety scene (left) is processed by \emph{neural} components (blue) that answer only local, checkable questions --- per-object predicates and open-vocabulary localization --- while a fixed \emph{symbolic} layer (amber) makes every safety-critical decision: hazard election by Eq.~\eqref{eq:fol} and routing into avoid / disengage / refuse regimes. Enforcement searches the frozen flow policy's noise space over $K{=}8$ candidate chunks, and a discrete-CBF chain re-checked on the executed prefix (green) certifies the emitted actions independently of which neural search produced them.}
\label{fig:pipeline}
\end{figure*}

Fig.~\ref{fig:pipeline} shows the assembled system. We describe the four layers in the order the runtime executes them.

\subsection{Hazard election: a three-literal first-order rule}
\label{sec:ident}

The scene-level decision is made by a fixed rule, not by a model:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\textsc{Hazard}(x) \coloneqq{}& \textsc{Protected}(x)\ \vee\ \textsc{Moving}(x)\ \vee \\
&\big(\lnot\textsc{Mentioned}(x)\wedge\textsc{NearPath}(x)\big),
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:fol}
\end{equation}
ranked protected-first, then by distance to the sanctioned reach path. $\textsc{Protected}$ covers a semantic class --- body parts and objects attached to a person --- that is never excluded by mention and is always floored onto the guard set. $\textsc{Moving}$ is grounded online by displacement across a settle window ($>$1\,cm) and re-read at every replan; this single literal is what handles dynamic human-hand intruders without any runtime model call. $\textsc{Mentioned}$ uses content-token overlap under a head-noun rule, and $\textsc{NearPath}$ measures distance to the sanctioned reach segments (end-effector $\rightarrow$ target $\rightarrow$ destination).

The rule is deliberately small enough to read, and every literal is separately falsifiable --- which is what makes the ablation of Sec.~\ref{sec:ablation} possible at all. Note what it is \emph{not}: it is not synthesized per scene, not retrieved, and not conditioned on a benchmark-specific table. On the affordance suite the danger literal is instantiated as a VLM-answered property predicate ($\textsc{Hot}$, $\textsc{Sharp}$) rather than $\textsc{NearPath}$; the routing is declared per suite in advance, not selected per episode.

\textbf{Two interchangeable grounding backends.} The literals can be grounded either (a) by hand heuristics plus one-shot cached VLM hazard-property priors with a fail-safe floor --- semantics may raise concern but never delete protection --- or (b) by \emph{VLM predicate grounding}, in which a small VLM (3 votes, cached per task, no runtime API call) answers only local per-object questions: $\textsc{Referenced}(x)$ and $\textsc{Protected}(x)$. On all 59 scenes of the two benchmarks the two backends produce the \emph{identical} election. The VLM backend additionally resolves references no string heuristic can: ``deliver it to me'' makes the hand $\textsc{Protected}$, and a plate held by a hand is correctly both $\textsc{Referenced}$ and $\textsc{Protected}$. This is the design finding in one sentence: \emph{the VLM answers local predicates; the logic makes the decision.}

\textbf{Geometric grounding.} At the perception tier, positions and extents come from open-vocabulary detection~\cite{gdino} back-projected through depth, with percentile-box extents (median localization error 0.067\,m on SafeLIBERO, 0.036--0.038\,m on LIBERO-Safety). Localization failures fall back conservatively and are logged per episode.

\subsection{Three-regime routing}
\label{sec:routing}

Not every hazard is a keep-out, and treating it as one is a failure mode we measured. The same predicate layer routes each episode:
(1) \textbf{Avoid} --- a geometric hazard off the task path; enforce as below.
(2) \textbf{Disengage} --- the hazard \emph{is} the destination ($\textsc{Protected}(x)\wedge\textsc{Referenced}(x)$, or the guard overlaps the parsed destination by $<$0.15\,m); the geometric keep-out is disengaged for the episode and safety comes from routing. This regime was forced on us by the human-handover suites, where any keep-out destroys the task (Sec.~\ref{sec:ls}).
(3) \textbf{Refuse} --- the \emph{instruction} is the hazard; a rule-based semantic judge over (verb, patient, hazard-property) triples refuses execution (offline F1 0.968, precision 0.938, recall 1.000; in-domain, see Sec.~\ref{sec:limitations}).
Routing and identification read the same predicates. This is one rule system, not three.

\subsection{Enforcement and certificate}
\label{sec:enforce}

Let $F(z,\mathrm{obs})$ be the frozen 10-step Euler denoise map and $M(a_{1:L})$ the minimum barrier-chain margin of the executed $L$-step prefix. All operators search the noise preimage $S=\{z: M(F(z))\ge0\}$, so every emitted action is $F(z^\star)$ --- exactly on the policy manifold. \textsc{Select} samples $K{=}8$ seeds sharing one vision-language KV cache and scores candidates lexicographically (feasibility $\succ$ margin $\succ$ progress); \textsc{Tilt} reweights and resamples the particle population at mid-denoising steps under soft margin potentials, at essentially no cost; \textsc{Ascend} takes reverse-mode gradient steps of the prefix margin through all ten Euler steps with respect to $z$ (57\,ms per step, backbone untouched). A soft annealed repulsor field on the velocity target is the natural soft complement, and is the only mechanism we found that navigates obstacle-on-grasp geometry, where every hard projection locks up.

The barrier is $B_j(p)=\min_{m,q}\lVert p+q-o_m\rVert-r_m(j)$ over obstacles $m$ and companion offsets $q$ (wrist, fingertip), inflated by $\lambda j$ to bound obstacle motion within a chunk; safety of a prefix is the discrete-CBF chain $B_j\ge(1-\gamma)B_{j-1}$, $\gamma=0.9$, whose geometric decay is textbook~\cite{agrawal2017dcbf} and for which we claim no novelty. What matters here is the acceptance semantics:

\begin{proposition}[Checked prefix certificate]\label{prop:cert}
The acceptance stage re-evaluates the chain on the executed prefix of the \emph{final decoded chunk} by explicit rollout of the actuation model. If the check passes, the prefix satisfies the chain regardless of how the chunk was produced. If all $K$ candidates fail, the argmax-margin candidate executes with its true margin reported --- fail-visible, not fail-silent.
\end{proposition}

Because soundness is a predicate of the emitted actions rather than of the search that produced them, the certificate covers \textsc{Select}, \textsc{Tilt}, \textsc{Ascend} and the repulsor identically. Correction-based arguments (``the repair enforces the chain'') cannot do this, and in fact prior in-denoising work does not re-verify its intermediate corrections on the final chunk at all. \emph{Stated non-claims}: rotation channels are uncertified; tracking error is absorbed into the safety margin rather than proved; we claim no formal perception-robustness radius, and quantify it empirically instead (Sec.~\ref{sec:e3}).

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:exp}

\emph{Privilege tiers.} Every claim is stated at a declared tier. \textbf{Tier-GT} reads hazard identity and object positions from the simulator --- the tier at which prior in-denoising work reports. \textbf{Tier-Percep} reads only the instruction, images, depth, proprioception and the symbolic object \emph{name} list; identity, positions, extents and corridor anchors are perception- or VLM-derived. Collision scoring is always ground-truth-anchored in both tiers. On SafeLIBERO a collision is $>$1\,mm ground-truth hazard displacement at any timestep; on LIBERO-Safety we use the benchmark's own \texttt{:constraints} predicates, with the important caveat of Sec.~\ref{sec:defect}.

\subsection{Identification: symbolic election matches ground truth}
\label{sec:e3}

Table~\ref{tab:e3} holds enforcement completely fixed and varies \emph{only} the identity source. Ground-truth-free election is statistically indistinguishable from privileged identity on every axis (paired exact McNemar, all $p\ge0.18$), and every trend favors the rule (pooled discordant episodes 12:6 in its favor). The no-GT identity tier therefore carries no measurable cost; what residual cost the perception tier does carry is \emph{geometric}, not semantic. Offline, the same rule wins on LIBERO-Safety --- 12/13 top-1 on the obstacle suite and 12/12 on the human suite, against 9/11 for a VLM-prior scorer, which pulls semantically alarming \emph{names} (ketchup, tomato sauce) above the benchmark's designated obstacle.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Identity-source swap on SafeLIBERO Spatial. Enforcement, seeds and initial states are identical across rows; only \emph{who names the hazard} changes. $n{=}40$/arm/level. Paired McNemar, GT vs.\ FOL: L1 TSR $p=0.55$, L1 CAR $p=0.50$, L2 TSR $p=0.18$, L2 CAR $p=1.0$.}
\label{tab:e3}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Identity source & L1 TSR/CAR & L2 TSR/CAR \\
\midrule
Ground truth (privileged) & 55.0 / 27.5 & 77.5 / 40.0 \\
VLM-prior scorer          & 60.0 / 27.5 & 77.5 / 37.5 \\
FOL rule (ours, no priors)& \textbf{62.5} / \textbf{32.5} & \textbf{90.0} / 40.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\textbf{Which direction do identification errors fail?} A deliberate-corruption study answers this and it is the uncomfortable answer: forcing the second-best identity pick costs collision avoidance ($70\rightarrow25$), not task success. Identification errors fail \emph{unsafe}. That is what motivates the documented top-2 guard-set mode, which recovers 19/19 recall under every corruption we injected, at $-12.5$ task-success points when engaged unnecessarily. We report the cost rather than defaulting the mode on.

