\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.80\textwidth]{fig_safelibero.pdf}
\caption{\textbf{From elected hazard to compiled barrier} on one SafeLIBERO scene. (a) The unguided policy drives through the hazard and topples the moka pot. (b) The same frozen weights under barrier-guided noise-space search complete the task with the hazard untouched. (c) The compiled constraint: the elected guard's barrier surface ($h{=}0$) and the sanctioned target (green). Nothing here is authored per scene --- the surface is compiled from the entity Eq.~\eqref{eq:fol} elects.}
\label{fig:sl}
\end{figure*}

Fig.~\ref{fig:sl} shows the mechanism on one scene, from elected hazard to compiled barrier. Table~\ref{tab:board} places five architectures on identical footing. The post-hoc row is unguided denoising plus one exact projection of the final chunk; the reimplementation row is our faithful re-run of~\cite{sota2607}'s method class ($K{=}1$, uniform schedule, privileged state, translational correction) scored under our criterion.\footnote{Referee note, stated plainly: the reimplementation's task-success uplift over baseline reproduces (up to $+17$pp at Spatial L2), but its published collision-avoidance figure does not reproduce at any swept obstacle radius under a $>$1\,mm criterion (best L2 CAR 70.5, at the cost of collapsing L1 TSR to 49.0). Their evaluation scores collisions ``by the terminal position of objects'' with no disclosed threshold; ours flags $>$1\,mm displacement at any timestep. We conclude the published number rests on a looser or terminal-only criterion, and present the honest comparison as ours-versus-our-own-reimplementation.}

Three findings. \emph{First}, post-hoc shielding is within $\pm$5--10 TSR and $\sim$7 CAR of in-denoising repair on every cell: moving the repair inside the denoising loop is not, by itself, the win. The axis that matters is repair versus search (Sec.~\ref{sec:intro}), not where the repair happens. \emph{Second}, the Long-horizon liveness stall afflicts every barrier-class shield (post-hoc 27.5 vs.\ baseline 58.0) and raising the step cap does not help; ours recovers $+15$--$19$ TSR while doubling CAR. \emph{Third}, the headline for this workshop is the last column: with \emph{no privileged state at all}, symbolic election plus perception dominates both shield architectures on both axes in 4/8 cells, and achieves task-success parity with 2--5$\times$ the collision avoidance in two more.

\subsection{LIBERO-Safety: porting the rule without tuning it}
\label{sec:ls}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{LIBERO-Safety, safety-finetuned \pifive{}, TSR/violation\%, paired seeds, $n{=}300$--750 per cell. Violations use the benchmark's own \texttt{:constraints} predicates. Two of the four violation columns carry no information and are marked rather than printed as zeros: human safety (\texttt{--}) because its sole predicate cannot fire (Sec.~\ref{sec:defect}), and affordance (\texttt{n/a}) because those task files declare \emph{no} \texttt{:constraints} block --- that suite expresses safety through its \emph{goal} predicate (grasp the handle, not the blade), so there is nothing to violate by construction. The two obstacle columns are live but measure only the object--hazard half of their conjunction. Macro TSR is unaffected throughout, since task success is scored by the goal predicate.}
\label{tab:ls}
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{2pt}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Arm & Obst. & Human & Obst+hand & Afford. & Macro TSR \\
\midrule
Baseline        & 73.6/4.7 & 85.8/-- & 69.3/5.3 & 56.7/n/a & 71.4 \\
Post-hoc shield & 75.3/2.7 & 37.3/-- & 46.0/2.0 & 51.3/n/a & 52.5 \\
SOTA-reimpl.    & 70.7/0.7 & 32.7/-- & 48.0/1.3 & 48.7/n/a & 50.0 \\
Ours (Tier-GT)  & \textbf{77.8}/4.2 & 78.7/-- & 63.2/1.9 & \textbf{58.7}/n/a & 69.6 \\
Ours (Percep)   & 68.7/\textbf{0.7} & \textbf{86.9}/-- & 58.9/1.7 & 56.0/n/a & 67.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The election rule transfers to a second benchmark with no tuning beyond the two class literals already in Eq.~\eqref{eq:fol}. Table~\ref{tab:ls} reports the five-arm board.

\textbf{We beat both dedicated shields by $+15$--$20$ macro task success} --- on the human suite alone they give up 48--54 task-success points ($p=1.8\times10^{-16}$ and $3.2\times10^{-19}$) --- \textbf{while matching or undercutting their violation rate on the two suites where that metric functions}. We state the halves separately on purpose: the human-suite $p$-values are task-success comparisons, and that suite's violation column is vacuous (Sec.~\ref{sec:defect}), so no safety claim rests on it. The per-suite story is the interesting one: both shields survive the static-obstacle suites and then \emph{halve} human-safety success (37.3 and 32.7 against our 86.9). That is safety-by-freezing versus safety-while-moving, and it is exactly what the disengage regime buys. Against the unfiltered baseline, our perception-tier violations run 12:1 over 750 paired episodes ($p=0.003$) at task-success parity on three of four suites, and the Tier-GT arm significantly exceeds baseline task success on obstacle avoidance (77.8 vs.\ 73.6, $n{=}450$, $p=0.045$).

\textbf{Disclosed against ourselves.} On obstacle+hand our task success is significantly \emph{below} baseline ($p=0.005$ and $0.03$ by tier) --- a freeze tax on cells where the intruder crosses the only viable approach. The baseline's successes on that suite carry a 5.3\% violation rate against our 1.7--1.9\%, which is the trade we are making, but it is a trade and not a win.

\textbf{The refusal track.} On the safety-reasoning suite the symbolic judge refuses 100/100/80\% of unsafe instructions with zero violations, against 80/72/36\% for the best published model, while the unguided policy \emph{executes} the same instructions at 94/60/100\%. That asymmetry --- fluent compliance, no judgment --- is the representation--action gap of Sec.~\ref{sec:gap} showing up at the level of whole tasks.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_fol_ablation.pdf}
\caption{\textbf{What each literal does, on one scene.} Guard sets produced by Eq.~\eqref{eq:fol} under single-clause deletion, for \emph{``pick the akita black bowl next to the cookies box and bring it for me.''} (a) The full rule guards the human hand and exempts the task bowls. (b) Without the logic, distance ranking guards the task bowl and \emph{misses the hand}. (c) Without the mention exemptions, the system guards the object it was told to pick up and paralyzes itself. (d) Without the class predicates the hand is left unguarded. Two of the three failures leave the true hazard unprotected; Table~\ref{tab:ablation} gives the rollout cost.}
\label{fig:ablation}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Every literal earns its place}
\label{sec:ablation}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Single-clause deletion from Eq.~\eqref{eq:fol}. Each arm deletes exactly one clause with everything else paired 1:1 on identical initial states, $n{=}150$/cell; exact McNemar. TSR (\%), with violation~\% shown only where it moves. The incumbent column is recomputed on the paired subset, so it differs slightly from Table~\ref{tab:ls}.}
\label{tab:ablation}
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{@{}llccl@{}}
\toprule
Deleted clause & Suite & Full & Ablated & $p$ \\
\midrule
all logic          & Obst.     & 68.7\,{\scriptsize(0.7)} & 44.0\,{\scriptsize(\textbf{4.7})} & 1.2e--6 \\
\;\;(guard nearest-$k$) & Obst+hand & 56.0 & 39.3 & 2.5e--4 \\
                   & Human     & 88.7 & 68.7 & 5.3e--6 \\
\midrule
$\lnot\textsc{Mentioned}$   & Obst.     & 68.7 & 40.0 & 1.8e--8 \\
\;\;exemptions     & Obst+hand & 56.0 & 39.3 & 6.2e--4 \\
\midrule
$\textsc{Prot}\vee\textsc{Mov}$ & Human & 88.7 & 82.0 & 0.10 \\
abstention         & Afford.   & 56.0 & 42.7 & --- \\
VLM property gnd.  & Afford.   & 56.0 & 47.3 & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

This is the defense against the natural objection that the barrier machinery does all the work and the logic is decoration. Fig.~\ref{fig:ablation} shows what each clause is holding up on a single scene; Table~\ref{tab:ablation} deletes one clause at a time and measures the rollout cost. Every significant arm confirms its pre-registered prediction, and one result deserves emphasis: \emph{geometry alone is not merely weaker, it is less safe}. Guarding the top-$k$ nearest-to-path objects with no logic at all raises violations from 0.7\% to 4.7\% ($p=1.2\times10^{-6}$) --- the only arm in the paper that moves violations the wrong way --- because distance ranking evicts the true hazard from the guard set in favor of a closer irrelevant object. The mention exemptions are worth $\approx$29 task-success points, since without them the system paralyzes itself against its own target. Abstention is worth $\approx$13: when the property predicate elects nothing, guarding nothing beats force-electing. And replacing VLM-grounded property tokens with hand-coded ones costs 8.7 points on the affordance suite --- the one place where the neural component's open vocabulary is doing measurable work.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_vlm_id_accuracy.pdf}
\caption{\textbf{How much authority should the neural component have?} Hazard-identification accuracy on the same 19 SafeLIBERO scenes, grouped by what the VLM is asked to do. Letting it \emph{decide the scene} (grey) or \emph{compose the rule} (orange) is worse than restricting it to \emph{grounding predicates} that a fixed rule composes (blue). Light extensions are the deployed top-2 guard set. The ordering, not any single bar, is the finding.}
\label{fig:vlmauth}
\end{figure}

\subsection{More neural authority fails, and fails unsafe}
\label{sec:negative}

We implemented, pre-registered and killed both natural escalations of VLM authority. Reporting them is not throat-clearing; they are what makes the division of labor a finding rather than a preference.

\textbf{Learned safety critics: prediction was never the bottleneck.} We trained a consequence critic to predict, from the current observation and a candidate action chunk, whether that chunk leads to hazard contact --- family (ii) of Sec.~\ref{sec:intro}. Offline it is good: contact AUC 0.93--0.999, and end-effector path prediction 8--25$\times$ more accurate than the single-integrator model our barrier assumes. Converting that into constrained selection over the $K$ decoded candidates nonetheless produces no rollout gain. On SafeLIBERO Spatial L1 ($n{=}200$, privileged tier) critic selection scores 64.5/28.5 TSR/CAR against 67.5/33.8 for the geometric method it was meant to replace and 67.0/14.0 unguided --- below both references on both axes. On LIBERO-Safety obstacle avoidance the composed critic scores 67.3 against 79.3 for a ten-line geometric engagement gate targeting the identical failure mode. The diagnosis is instructive: in 74.5\% of replans the feasible set is empty and the certified fallback carries the safety anyway, so selection pressure is too weak to matter, and with marginal ranking quality (Spearman 0.597) always-ranking is net negative. A critic that predicts consequences accurately still needs something to select \emph{among}, and at this data scale the symbolic gate supplies more decision value than the learned one.

\textbf{Whole-scene VLM identification.} Fig.~\ref{fig:vlmauth} places all three levels of neural authority on one axis, over the same 19 SafeLIBERO scenes. Handing the whole scene \emph{and its images} to an API-strength VLM and asking it to name the hazard scores 84.2\% (GPT-5.5), 57.9\% (Claude Sonnet) and 47.4\% (Gemini-2.5-Flash), against 100\% for the decomposed rule. This is the configuration most favourable to the baseline --- images provided, strongest available models --- and the decomposition claim survives it.

\textbf{Runtime VLM rule composition.} Letting a VLM \emph{compose} the rule from a predicate library is strictly worse than the fixed rule with real critics from two vendors (top-1/top-2 of 19 scenes: 13/16 for Haiku, 11/13 for Gemini, against 16/19 and 19/19 for the fixed rule over VLM-grounded predicates), and even with \emph{oracle} grounding the entire compositional apparatus gains at most one scene and loses one to selection brittleness. The characteristic failure is the dangerous one: over-restrictive conjunctions that fire on \emph{nothing} (2 and 10 empty-rule scenes respectively), leaving the true hazard unguarded. A composition system that fails closed would merely be conservative; this one fails open.

\textbf{Scene graphs: no-go, three times.} Graph-structured identification exactly ties the flat scorer wherever hazards are objects (19/19 agreement on SafeLIBERO) and collapses identically on the relational human suite (0/2), where the actual fix was a ten-line $\textsc{Protected}$ class rule (12/12). Structure was not the missing ingredient; a class predicate was.

\section{A Benchmark Reliability Finding}
\label{sec:defect}

We close with a defect we did not go looking for. It surfaced because two of our own measurements on the same checkpoint and the same scenes disagreed: an independent geom-level scorer found 32.0\% robot--hand contact on the human-safety suite, while the benchmark's own violation metric reported 0\% for every arm, ours and baselines alike.

The cause is in \texttt{check\_robot\_contact}, which collects the robot's geoms as integer \emph{indices},
\begin{center}\small\texttt{g\_group.append(geom\_i)}\end{center}
and passes them to \texttt{\_check\_contact}, which tests membership by \emph{name}:
\begin{center}\small\texttt{c1\_in\_g1 = geom\_1\_name in geoms\_1}\end{center}
A string is never a member of a list of integers, and both disjuncts of the acceptance test require a term drawn from that list, so the function returns \texttt{False} unconditionally. \texttt{CheckRobotContact} has never fired, in any suite, for any method evaluated on this benchmark. We supply a standalone executable proof that needs no simulator: a fake contact buffer in which a group-0 gripper geom and the hazard geom touch in both orderings returns \texttt{False} as shipped and \texttt{True} when indices are replaced by names. The fix is that one substitution. The defect is isolated --- the object--object \texttt{CheckContact} path and the gripper-part predicates pass names and are correct --- and it is specific to this benchmark, which introduced the predicate.

\textbf{What it costs us, stated plainly.} Our LIBERO-Safety violation numbers measure object--hazard contact only. The human-safety suite's sole constraint is \texttt{CheckRobotContact}, so its violation rate is identically zero \emph{by construction}, which is why that column is struck through in Table~\ref{tab:ls}. Any reading of the form ``the baseline never touches the hand, so the trained behavior is already safe'' --- a reading we ourselves held --- is an artifact and is retracted here. The disengage regime remains motivated, but by the real task-success collapse under keep-out (2--26\% against a 54/56/26 baseline), not by a zero-violation baseline. Our macro violation rates average in a structurally-zero column and are diluted accordingly; the 12:1 paired violation result is carried by the remaining three suites. SafeLIBERO is unaffected, since its criterion is ground-truth hazard displacement computed independently of these predicates. Re-scoring our stored rollouts under the corrected predicate requires per-step contact traces we did not log, and is the first thing we will run.

\textbf{Why this belongs in this workshop.} This is the third independent defect we have found in one safety benchmark, alongside a 25\,cm obstacle-spawn shift between twin variants that moves the dependent variable, and an unparseable goal predicate that silently zeroes three tasks. Each is a small piece of unchecked imperative code sitting where a safety-critical decision is made. The argument for putting such decisions in a small, inspectable, individually-testable symbolic form is not aesthetic. A three-literal rule whose every clause we could delete and measure is a rule whose failure modes we found; a predicate buried in a benchmark's contact-checking utility is one nobody found for as long as it has existed.

\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\textbf{Honesty items.} All SafeLIBERO comparisons against the state-of-the-art method are against our own faithful reimplementation under one criterion, because the published collision figure does not reproduce under ours at any swept radius. All LIBERO-Safety claims are paired against our self-run baseline on the released checkpoint: that checkpoint reproduces the benchmark's protocol mechanics in our harness but scores well below the published obstacle-avoidance numbers, the release carries no model card, and we could not resolve provenance from our side. The refusal judge's lexicon was written against this suite's vocabulary, so its F1 is an in-domain upper bound. The perception tier consumes the symbolic object \emph{name} list; dropping it costs identification (top-2 recall $19\rightarrow12$ of 19) and pixel-only naming is future work.

\textbf{Structural limitations.} Obstacle-on-grasp geometry resists every hard mechanism we tried, with the soft repulsor's 50\% the current ceiling. Identity errors fail unsafe at $k{=}1$, mitigated but not removed by the guard-set mode. Rotation is uncertified. We extract part-level semantics (blade versus handle) but ground barriers at object level, because part perception fails our pre-registered side-discrimination gate --- the semantic layer is ahead of what perception can ground, which we consider the most interesting thing blocking the next version. Benchmark-definition divergence is real and shows in Table~\ref{tab:board}: a semantically correct hazard model can disagree with a benchmark's designated label.

\textbf{Toward hardware.} Our margins are tuned to simulation-grade localization (3.6--6.7\,cm median error); the obstacle-motion assumption bounds movers at $\le$8\,cm/s at 20\,Hz, which is below natural hand speeds; and tracking error is absorbed into the safety margin rather than proved for a specific controller. None of these is an obstacle in principle, and all three are unproved here.

\section{Conclusion}

The safety filter's four modules all presuppose that someone has named the hazard. For frozen VLA policies, naming it is the hard part, and it is neither elicitable from the policy nor installable by prompting: the hazard is linearly decodable at the action interface at AUC 0.998 in a policy that cannot answer whether there is a table in front of it and that 115 cells of instruction rewriting cannot redirect. Our answer is a division of labor in which neural components ground local, checkable predicates and a fixed three-literal rule makes every safety-critical decision --- a division we defend by deleting each literal and measuring what breaks, and by showing that both ways of granting the neural component more authority fail unsafe. That the same paper had to retract one of its own claims because a benchmark's central safety predicate silently returns false is not an aside. It is the argument.

\section*{Reproducibility}
The standalone proof of the \texttt{CheckRobotContact} defect runs in isolation with no simulator, GPU, or benchmark installation, and is included with the submission.

